1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,320
Imagine the sun vanished

2
00:00:01,320 --> 00:00:02,570
right now.

3
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:04,490
For about eight minutes,

4
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:05,830
you wouldn't know.

5
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,350
This can't actually happen,

6
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:09,680
but the delay is

7
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:10,170
real.

8
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:12,280
Sunlight takes about eight

9
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:13,540
minutes and 20 seconds

10
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:14,530
to reach Earth,

11
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:16,040
so the light arriving

12
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:17,540
now left the sun

13
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:18,510
minutes ago.

14
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:20,500
Remove the source, and

15
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:21,840
that last light still

16
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:23,310
has distance to cross.

17
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:25,590
Then, daylight ends.

18
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:27,380
Here's the stranger part.

19
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:29,250
In this thought experiment,

20
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:30,880
Earth's orbit wouldn't change

21
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:32,110
immediately either.

22
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:33,840
Changes in gravity also

23
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:35,160
propagate at the speed

24
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:35,710
of light.

25
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:37,650
Once that change arrived,

26
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:38,920
Earth would leave its

27
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:40,170
old solar orbit.

28
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:41,920
So our first warning

29
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,030
wouldn't be instant.

30
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:44,660
We'd spend those final

31
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:46,240
minutes living in the

32
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:47,270
sun's past.

